W54 M15 — equivalence evidence for the four survivors that had no re-test (G05, W11, N39, N40).

Method and M15 compliance
  Every run below is the row's own in-memory method: a Vite transform plugin replaces ONE text in ONE
  module while it is transformed, and (for these measurements only) appends ONE candidate test to a
  test module. Both happen in memory. The config, the candidate tests and the markers live outside the
  repository, under /var/folders/.../T/kilo/w54_repair/. Nothing under apps/** or packages/** was
  written, edited or generated by any of this: `git status` over the row's folders is unchanged from the
  state the row left it in, and .cache/w54/mut/mutations.json kept its md5 98b88e1b2fa51d849bf8623bbfc3ff2f.
  A run counts only when the plugin left its marker (the mutation was really applied); each block below
  says which markers exist. Commands were run from /Users/gsp-budi/Documents/DEMS on 2026-10-05 (WIB),
  --maxWorkers=1, one file at a time. No credential, token, cookie value or password appears below.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
G05  routes/_app/admin/-guard.ts — "the redirect leaves a history entry"
     find `throw redirect({ to: '/dashboard', replace: true })` → `throw redirect({ to: '/dashboard' })`
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Three surfaces were measured, each at baseline and with G05 applied. A candidate test was appended to
src/test/live-shell-admin.test.tsx. The candidate's own assertion failed at BASELINE too (lengthAfter=2
against an expected 1), which is itself part of the finding: the entry the router leaves comes from the
navigation attempt, not from the redirect's replace flag.

  (a) memory history, client-side navigation to the refused page
      baseline : PROBE_G05 pathname=/dashboard lengthBefore=1 lengthAfter=2
      G05      : PROBE_G05 pathname=/dashboard lengthBefore=1 lengthAfter=2   [marker G05]

  (b) memory history, full traversal (six observables)
      baseline : {"mounted":"len=1 path=/dashboard",
                  "afterRefusedNavigation":"len=2 path=/dashboard",
                  "afterBack":"len=2 path=/dashboard",
                  "afterForward":"len=2 path=/dashboard",
                  "directMount":"len=1 path=/dashboard",
                  "directMountBack":"len=1 path=/dashboard"}
      G05      : byte-identical to baseline                                  [marker G05]

  (c) BROWSER history (production's implementation, jsdom window.history), so the result does not
      depend on any quirk of createMemoryHistory
      baseline : PROBE_G05c mountedLen=1 afterRefusedNavLen=2 afterBackPath=/dashboard backLen=2
      G05      : PROBE_G05c mountedLen=1 afterRefusedNavLen=2 afterBackPath=/dashboard backLen=2  [marker G05]

  Verdict: EQUIVALENT MUTANT for every test surface available to the row. `replace` changes nothing
  observable here: the refused page cannot be reached at all, and Back over it re-runs the same guard,
  which lands on /dashboard either way. What the flag decides is how many dead entries a real browser
  keeps between /dashboard and the page before it — not observable in jsdom (measured above), and
  masked in a real browser by the guard running again. Pinning it would mean asserting the source text
  (a text pin, not a behaviour) or a lane Back-press check in a real browser, which is outside M15.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
W11  components/shell/mode-switch.tsx — "a second press while switching sends a second switch"
     find `if (control === null || target === null || switching) return` → without `|| switching`
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
The row's own test already presses the confirm button twice while the switch runs ("Live: the chip opens
the confirmation for Simulated and hands the choice to the runtime once", src/test/live-shell-indicator
.test.tsx:326-347, with a switchTo that never settles). It cannot see W11. Reason, measured:

  (a) attributes of the confirm button after the first press, and the number of switchTo calls
      baseline : attrs={"aria-disabled":"true","data-pending":"true","data-pressed":null,
                        "disabled":false,"pendingAttrNames":["data-pending"]} switchToCalls=1
      W11      : attrs={"aria-disabled":"true","data-pending":"true","data-pressed":null,
                        "disabled":false,"pendingAttrNames":["data-pending"]} switchToCalls=1  [marker W11]
      → the kit renders the button pending and aria-disabled from the moment switching is true, so the
        second press never reaches `confirm`. The deleted clause is unreachable through the UI.

  To show that the invariant IS pinned and that the two guards are redundant protections of it, a
  second, synthetic mutation K01 was applied alongside (K01 removes the OTHER guard, `isPending={switching}`,
  from the same button; it is not one of the row's 137 mutations and is used here only as a probe):

  (b) K01 alone (pending removed, the `switching` clause kept)
      Tests  22 passed (22)          → the guard alone holds the invariant.        [marker K01]
  (c) W11 + K01 together (both guards gone)
      × Live: the chip opens the confirmation for Simulated and hands the choice to the runtime once
      Tests  1 failed | 21 passed (22) → the row's EXISTING test catches the double switch the moment
                                        both guards are gone. [markers K01 W11]

  Verdict: EQUIVALENT MUTANT by redundancy. Two independent guards protect one invariant; removing
  either alone is unobservable, removing both fails a test the row already has. No test can kill W11
  without first removing the guard that makes it equivalent — and that guard is the row's own code.

