N39  components/shell/live-notices.tsx — "the first comparison after a sign-in reports a change"
     find `if (session === null || previous.session === null) return` → `if (session === null) return`
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
The mutant needs a mounted LiveNoticesBody whose previously known session was null and whose session is
now set. Under the mutant that path dereferences a null `before` (`before.operator.id`) and would throw.
Measured, appending a candidate test to src/test/live-shell-notices.test.tsx that mounts the console
signed out — the only way to make `previous.session` null:

      baseline : PROBE_N39 path=/login appShellAbsent=true noticesMounted=false roleNoticeCalls=0
      N39      : PROBE_N39 path=/login appShellAbsent=true noticesMounted=false roleNoticeCalls=0  [marker N39]

  The signed-out console is redirected to /login by the `_app` guard, so AppShell — the only mount
  point of LiveNotices — is never mounted, and no role notice is ever called for. The state the mutant
  needs cannot be produced through any surface the row owns, because of an invariant of W50's runtime:
  auth reaches SIGNED_IN and the session becomes known in ONE store update
  (packages/shared/src/live/source/live-sim.ts:33, "The session is known ... AND the snapshot ... both
  in ONE store update | SIGNED_IN"), and the shell mounts only when signed in.

  Verdict: EQUIVALENT MUTANT within every state the row's surfaces can reach. `previous.session === null`
  is defensive against a state the runtime's own contract forbids. A test could kill it only by
  rendering the hook outside the router with a hand-made source that breaks that invariant — a test of
  a state production cannot produce, offered here as optional hardening, not as the row's gap.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
N40  components/shell/live-notices.tsx — "the notices are rendered in Simulated mode"
     find `return live === null ? null : <LiveNoticesBody live={live} active={active} />`
     replace `return <LiveNoticesBody live={live as WebLive} active={active} />`
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
The two branches differ only when `useWebLive()` returns null, i.e. only in Simulated mode. Measured
there, with a candidate test appended to src/test/live-shell-admin.test.tsx that also presses the page's
own "Switch to Live mode" — the strongest observable in that mode, since a mounted notice body carries
its own mode-switch dialog:

      baseline : PROBE_N40 path=/admin/users toastSlots=0 lostTitles=0 chip=SIMULATED dialogs=1 switchToCalls=1
      N40      : PROBE_N40 path=/admin/users toastSlots=0 lostTitles=0 chip=SIMULATED dialogs=1 switchToCalls=1  [marker N40]

  Why nothing differs: with no Live source, every hook the body uses reads the same empty context —
  `useLiveLink()` gives null and `readSession(null)` gives null; `isLinkLost(null)` is false, which
  short-circuits the ONLY dereference of `live` (`live.socketStatus()`, live-notices.tsx:58, on the right
  of `&&`); `down` therefore stays false, `showing = active && down` is false, so LinkLostToast is never
  mounted (live-notices.tsx:74) and `dialog` is null while `target === null`. The body renders nothing
  but two inert hook calls: no element, no toast, no dialog, no socket, no subscription.

  Verdict: EQUIVALENT MUTANT. The ternary is a type-level narrowing that lets LiveNoticesBody take a
  non-null `live`, not a branch in behaviour. No assertion over observable behaviour separates it.

